\vspace{8pt}

\noindent Because  $\Gamma (y_2) \cap V(C)=3$, we are only left to deal with the last two possibilities of Table 1 for $\Gamma (y_2) \cap V(C)$. In both possibilities, $\Gamma (y_1) \cap V(C)$ will induce a $C_3$ by Lemma \ref{l2}(b). In the first possibility, $\Gamma (y_1) \cap V(C)=\{u_1,u_5,u_9\}$  gives rise to the 15-cycle given by  $(u_1,u_5,...,u_8,y_2,y,x,y_1, u_9,...,u_{14},u_1)$, a contradiction.  In the second possibility, $\Gamma (y_1) \cap V(C)=\{u_1,u_5,u_{10}\}$  gives rise to the 15-cycle given by $ (u_1,u_5,u_6,...,u_8,y_2,z,y,x,y_1,u_{10},...,u_{14},u_1)$, a contradiction. 



\vspace{8pt}

\noindent In the case $y_1,y_2 \in K'$, since $y_1$ and $y_2$ are connected by paths of lengths 2, 3 or 4 in $H$, no pair of vertices selected from of $\Gamma (y_1) \cap V(C)$ and  $\Gamma (y_2) \cap V(C)$ can be separated by a path of length 3, 4 or 5 along the cycle $C$. The cardinality of the possible vertex set of  $\Gamma (y_1) \cap V(C)$ is presented in Table 2 and each of these leads to a contradiction as $\Gamma (y_2) \cap V(C)<3$

